# TT.UTIL -- close_old_dev_tickets (S-UTIL-010..014): PRAGMA AUTONOMOUS_TRANSACTION with a
# COMMIT per closed ticket on Oracle, a plain PROCEDURE with a real COMMIT on PostgreSQL
# (util.FINDINGS.md, Checklist dimension 5, option 1) -- hence COMMITS=yes: the PostgreSQL side
# runs without a transaction block, the Oracle side is followed by cleanup.ora.sql.
#
# The routine acts only on a "dev" instance (S-UTIL-011/012). Oracle decides with
# `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM V$INSTANCE WHERE HOST_NAME LIKE '%-dev-%'`; the container's host name
# is its Docker id (43ac78e32526 on 2026-10-21), so Oracle ROLLBACKs its (empty) autonomous
# transaction and RETURNs. PostgreSQL decides with the GUC mcdb.instance_role = 'dev' (or the
# test-only mcdb.force_dev_instance = 'true'); neither is set on mcdb_tmpl or its clones
# (pg_db_role_setting is empty), so the translation ROLLBACKs and RETURNs as well. This
# scenario therefore PROVES THE NO-OP on both engines: an eligible abandoned MCC ticket (hot,
# handled_by_mcc = 1, opened 40 days ago) keeps its status, no event is written, and the
# deciding condition is probed on each side (probe.ora.sql / probe.pg.sql: is_dev = 0). The
# dev path itself (change_status to 102 + COMMIT per ticket, despam_ticket over 5000 events)
# cannot be compared here: no dev Oracle instance is available.
#
# setup.ora.sql COMMITs the three fixture tickets so that Oracle's autonomous transaction
# could see them if the instance were a dev instance (an autonomous transaction does not see
# the caller's uncommitted rows); cleanup.ora.sql removes them (and anything a dev path would
# have written) afterwards. The fixtures touch tt.ticket only (no company/contact/host), so
# ORA_USER=tt suffices and the probe table is TT's own GTT.
ORA_USER=tt
COMMITS=yes
COMPARE_MSG=no
DESCRIPTION=close_old_dev_tickets is a no-op on a non-dev instance on both engines (is_dev = 0 probed), eligible ticket 958400 untouched
